1. e4 {Takes the centre and opens lines for the queen and the light-squared bishop.}
1... e5 {Black claims an equal share of the centre.}
2. Nf3 {Develops with an attack on e5.}
  ( 2. Qh5?! {Bringing the queen out this early lets Black develop with tempo.}
    2... Nc6 3. Bc4 g6 4. Qf3 Nf6 {Black is already ahead in development.} )
2... Nc6 {The most natural way to defend e5.}
  ( 2... f6? {Weakens the king and takes the knight's best square.}
    3. Nxe5! fxe5?? 4. Qh5+ Ke7 5. Qxe5+ Kf7 6. Bc4+ {White's attack is decisive.} )
  ( 2... d6 {The Philidor Defence: solid but a little passive.}
    3. d4 Nf6 4. Nc3 Nbd7 5. Bc4 {White develops naturally and keeps a pleasant edge.} )
3. Bc4 {The Italian Game. The bishop aims at f7, the weakest point in Black's camp.}
  ( 3. Nxe5? {Grabbing the pawn just loses the knight.} 3... Nxe5 )
3... Bc5 {The Giuoco Piano.}
  ( 3... Nf6 {The Two Knights Defence.}
    4. d3 {A quiet, solid choice.} 4... Be7 5. O-O O-O 6. Re1 d6
    7. c3 {White prepares d4 under good conditions.} )
  ( 3... Nd4?! {The Blackburne Shilling Gambit sets a trap but is objectively dubious.}
    4. Nxd4 {Simplest.}
      ( 4. Nxe5?? {Takes the bait.} 4... Qg5! 5. Nxf7 Qxg2 6. Rf1 Qxe4+ 7. Be2 Nf3# )
    4... exd4 5. O-O {White is ahead in development.} )
4. c3 {Preparing d4 to build a big centre.}
4... Nf6 5. d4 exd4 6. cxd4 Bb4+ 7. Bd2 Bxd2+ 8. Nbxd2
8... d5 {Black hits back in the centre before White can consolidate.} *
